const faqSchema = {
 "@context": "https://schema.org",
 "@type": "FAQPage",
 "mainEntity": [
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is cname flattening in simple terms?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Think of it as the rulebook that a specific part of the internet follows. Every page in this section explains one rule, why it exists, and how to follow it correctly."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Do I need to memorize all of this?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "No: bookmark this page and the related tools. The goal is understanding what to check and when, not memorizing syntax."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "How long does it take to implement?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Most configurations take 5-15 minutes in your DNS panel. The verification (using our tools) takes another 2 minutes."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What happens if I get it wrong?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Depends on the setting: some errors cause immediate visible failures, others silently degrade deliverability or security. Every guide includes the symptoms to watch for."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Is this the same across all DNS providers?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "The underlying protocol is identical; the dashboard layout and terminology differ. Our provider playbooks cover the exact flow for Cloudflare, GoDaddy, Namecheap, Route 53, and others."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Where can I get help if I'm stuck?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Each guide links to the relevant free tool so you can verify your work. The support page lists email channels for questions the guides don't cover."
   }
  }
 ]
}
---
<BaseLayout title="CNAME Flattening Explained (Cloudflare and Alternatives)" description="CNAME flattening explained: how providers resolve apex CNAMEs into A records, on-query vs on-publish modes, and when to use it."  schema={[faqSchema]}
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a> <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/dns-records/" class="hover:text-accent">Record Types</a> <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">CNAME Flattening</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">CNAME Flattening Explained</h1>
    <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
      The zone apex cannot hold a CNAME, but modern hosts want you to point the apex at a hostname.
      Flattening resolves that contradiction: the provider quietly resolves the target and answers
      with plain A records. You keep CNAME ergonomics; the internet sees legal DNS.
    </p>
    <div class="rich-text mt-8">
      <h2>How it works</h2>
      <p>
        You enter a CNAME at the root, something RFC rules technically forbid. Your DNS host, at
        query time or publish time, resolves that target itself and returns the resulting IP
        addresses as if they were ordinary A records. Cloudflare does this automatically for any apex
        CNAME; Route 53 and DNSimple expose it as ALIAS records; some hosts offer both modes.
      </p>
      <h2>On-query vs on-publish</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60">
        <table class="pdns-table">
          <thead><tr><th></th><th>Flatten on query</th><th>Flatten on publish</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Freshness</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Always current</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Current as of last refresh + TTL</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Speed</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Slightly slower (resolution at edge)</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Fastest</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Failure mode</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Depends on provider resolvers</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Stale IPs after target changes</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <h2>When to use it</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Pointing the apex at CDNs, load balancers, and PaaS hosts that give you a hostname, not an IP.</li>
        <li>Escaping origin IPs that rotate without notice.</li>
        <li>Anywhere you would use a <a href="/cname-record/">CNAME</a> if the name weren't the root.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>The caveats</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>It is a provider feature, not portable DNS: moving your zone elsewhere means re-creating the setup there.</li>
        <li>Only address records flatten; TXT verification living at the target does not transfer to your apex.</li>
        <li>Keep TTLs modest (300) so answers track a moving target.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>Verify yours</h2>
      <p>
        An A-record <a href="/dns-record-lookup/">lookup</a> on your apex returns the flattened
        addresses; a CNAME lookup shows whether your provider exposes the underlying alias.
      </p>
      
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 grid gap-3"><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">What is cname flattening in simple terms?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Think of it as the rulebook that a specific part of the internet follows. Every page in this section explains one rule, why it exists, and how to follow it correctly.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Do I need to memorize all of this?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">No: bookmark this page and the related tools. The goal is understanding what to check and when, not memorizing syntax.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">How long does it take to implement?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Most configurations take 5-15 minutes in your DNS panel. The verification (using our tools) takes another 2 minutes.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">What happens if I get it wrong?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Depends on the setting: some errors cause immediate visible failures, others silently degrade deliverability or security. Every guide includes the symptoms to watch for.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Is this the same across all DNS providers?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">The underlying protocol is identical; the dashboard layout and terminology differ. Our provider playbooks cover the exact flow for Cloudflare, GoDaddy, Namecheap, Route 53, and others.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Where can I get help if I'm stuck?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Each guide links to the relevant free tool so you can verify your work. The support page lists email channels for questions the guides don't cover.</p></details></div>
<h2>Related</h2>
